Genome Intelligence Atlas: Mathematical Foundations of the Genotype–Cognition Relationship

Version 3.0 — A Comprehensive Theoretical PhD Proposal

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Claude, Gemini, Deepseek, kimi 

Abstract

This document presents the integrated mathematical blueprint for the Genome Intelligence Atlas—a layered, open ontology that spans genomic variation, gene–environment interplay, molecular networks, brain development, neural computation, cognitive modelling, and artificial intelligence synthesis. It addresses a fundamental challenge: how to structure, verify, and propagate knowledge about the biological roots of cognition under explicit, quantified uncertainty, while embedding ethical and population‑level constraints directly into the formal architecture. Building on statistical genetics, stochastic processes, Bayesian inference, information theory, causal reasoning, and multi‑agent learning, we introduce original contributions in (i) feedback‑corrected cross‑layer information bounds, (ii) formalization of genotype–environment interaction within polygenic scores, (iii) mathematical justification for rejecting individual‑level prediction, (iv) probabilistic hypergraphs for scientific evidence with convergence guarantees, (v) multi‑agent consensus with contradiction detection and adversarial robustness, and (vi) tiered causal identification. All claims are accompanied by explicit hypotheses, proof sketches, and non‑causality flags. The ethical commitment to non‑engineering and non‑discrimination is structurally enforced. This document serves as the theoretical foundation for a doctoral programme devoted to understanding—never modifying—the human basis of intelligence.

---

1. Introduction: Vision, Scope, and Ethical Encoding

The scientific objective of the Genome Intelligence Atlas is to construct an open, collaborative mathematical knowledge base that captures the statistical and mechanistic relationships between the human genome, epigenetic regulation, brain development, neural computation, learning, and cognition. The Atlas is not a predictive tool for individuals; it is a population‑level reasoning platform designed to expose the structure, limits, and uncertainty of genotype–cognition associations. This document defines the rigorous mathematical framework that will underpin the Atlas, integrating existing well‑established results with novel theory developed in the thesis.

Operationalized Ethical Safeguards

1. No individual‑level prediction. The system architecture prohibits any module that maps genotypes to individual cognitive outcomes. The mathematical basis for this prohibition is formalised in Section 3.6, where we prove that even under optimal linear prediction, the individual prediction interval is far too wide to be scientifically or ethically defensible.
2. Tiered non‑causality flag. Every quantitative claim carries a causal tier label—statistical association, Mendelian randomization evidence, or experimentally validated mechanism. Only claims in the third tier may be considered causal; all others are explicitly flagged as non‑causal unless derived from a formally identified do‑calculus adjustment.
3. Explicit uncertainty. All models provide credible intervals, mutual information bounds, and prediction intervals where relevant.
4. Socioeconomic confound awareness. The Atlas must recognise and model the confounding effect of socioeconomic status (SES) and genetic nurture (Section 1.1). Queries that could be used to justify resource allocation based on genetic scores are refused.
5. Open‑source, versioned, and auditable. The entire knowledge graph, codebase, and benchmarks will be open‑source under a license that prohibits individual‑prediction applications.

Document Structure. We fix global notation in Section 2. Sections 3–8 detail the six layers with rigorous formulations, now extended with new modules for G×E, population stratification, individual uncertainty, rare variants, epigenetics, developmental timing, and cross‑species evidence. Section 9 presents the feedback‑corrected information bound. Section 10 formalizes the knowledge graph as a probabilistic hypergraph with replication weighting and convergence proofs. Section 11 develops multi‑agent reasoning theory with adversarial robustness and dynamic versioning. Section 12 outlines the validation, falsification, and benchmarking framework. Section 13 summarizes the original contributions. Interoperability standards are addressed in Appendix F.

