4. Layer 2 — Molecular Networks (Extended)

4.1 Deterministic Core and Its Limitation (original)

Gene regulatory dynamics are given by

\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \mathbf{W}\,\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) - \mathbf{D}\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{u}. \tag{4}

When copy numbers are low, stochasticity dominates; the protein coefficient of variation from a two‑stage burst model is

\mathrm{CV}^2_P = \frac{1}{\langle P\rangle} + \frac{\langle B\rangle}{\langle P\rangle}\cdot\frac{\tau_{1/2,\text{mRNA}}}{\tau_{1/2,\text{protein}}}. \tag{5}

(Thattai & van Oudenaarden, 2001). This domain boundary is flagged.

4.2 Edge Uncertainty in Regulatory Networks (original)

Each regulatory edge probability p_{ij} comes from Bayesian model averaging. Ensemble stochastic simulations propagate this uncertainty.

4.3 Epigenetic and Parental Inheritance Bridge (New Contribution)

Epigenetic marks (DNA methylation, histone modifications) constitute an additional layer of heritable information that is not captured by DNA sequence. We introduce an epigenetic state vector \mathbf{e}(t) that evolves according to

\frac{d\mathbf{e}}{dt} = \mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{e},t) + \boldsymbol{\eta}(t),

where \boldsymbol{\eta} is a stochastic term capturing methylation drift and environmental influences. The information flow from genome to cognition is then bounded by

I(\text{DNA};\text{Cognition}) \le I(\text{DNA},\text{Epi};\text{Cognition}) - I(\text{Epi};\text{Cognition}\mid\text{DNA}).

Since I(\text{Epi};\text{Cog}\mid\text{DNA}) \ge 0, ignoring epigenetics underestimates the true biological information channel. Furthermore, mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) contributes to ATP production and synaptic plasticity; we treat mtDNA variants as an additional genetic component with maternal inheritance. The combined genetic–epigenetic–mtDNA information bound will be derived in Section 9 with feedback.

