5. Layer 3 — Brain Development (Extended)

5.1 Stem Cell Proliferation (original)

The branching process with division rates \lambda_S,\lambda_P and death rates gives expected neuron count

\langle N(t)\rangle = \frac{\lambda_S\delta}{\mu_P-\lambda_P}\left(e^{(\lambda_P-\mu_P)t}-1\right). \tag{6}

5.2 Axon Guidance via Langevin Equation (original)

Growth cone dynamics follow

d\mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x}_t)dt + \sqrt{2D}\,d\mathbf{W}_t, \tag{7}

with Fokker–Planck equation

\partial_t p = -\nabla\cdot(\mathbf{v}p) + D\nabla^2 p. \tag{8}

Parameter estimation from observed trajectories uses the Onsager–Machlup action functional.

5.3 Developmental Timing and Critical Periods (New Contribution)

Genetic effects are not static; the same variant may have different impacts depending on developmental stage. We extend the branching model to age‑dependent rates \lambda_S(t),\lambda_P(t),\delta(t). A sensitive period is defined as a time window [\tau_1,\tau_2] during which the dynamical system is close to a bifurcation point, rendering it hypersensitive to parameter perturbations. Formally, a bifurcation occurs when the linearized Jacobian has an eigenvalue crossing the imaginary axis. We define a sensitivity index \mathcal{S}(t) as the derivative of the final neuron number with respect to a parameter \theta (e.g., a gene expression level) integrated over the critical window.

Theorem (Critical Period Information Bound). For any fixed genetic variant, the mutual information between its allele and adult cognition satisfies

I(\text{SNP};\text{Cognition}) \le \max_{\tau} I(\text{SNP}; \text{Brain State at } \tau),

and the bound is tightest during critical periods when small genetic differences lead to large structural divergence. Proof sketch. Using the data‑processing inequality, any path from gene to adult cognition passes through intermediate brain states. The mutual information is bounded by the bottleneck with the smallest capacity; this bottleneck is found at developmental windows where the gene–brain association is strongest. The claim follows from the functional relationship between brain state and downstream cognition.

5.4 Cross‑Species Translation Layer (New Contribution)

Many mechanistic links are validated in model organisms (mouse, zebrafish, C. elegans). We define an orthology mapping \mathcal{O}(g,s) and a conservation score c(g,s)\in[0,1] based on sequence identity and functional domain conservation. The evolutionary prior for a human claim given evidence from species s is

P(\text{claim true in human} \mid \text{evidence in } s) = f(c(g,s), d_{\text{phylo}}, \text{pheno}_{\text{sim}}),

where d_{\text{phylo}} is phylogenetic distance and \text{pheno}_{\text{sim}} measures phenotypic similarity of the relevant trait. A default Bayesian model sets the log‑odds additive in conservation and distance. All edges in the knowledge graph are tagged with the species from which the evidence originates, and the hypergraph inference (Section 10) down‑weights model organism evidence according to this prior.

