8. Layer 6 — AI Integration and Multi‑Agent Theory (Extended)

8.1 Architecture (original)

Four specialised agents—KG agent, search agent, reasoning agent, verification agent—operate over a shared probabilistic knowledge graph.

8.2 Multi‑Agent Bayesian Consensus (original, extended)

Agent i maintains a belief distribution q_i^{(t)}(\theta) over a latent claim \theta. Upon receiving neighbour j’s belief, the agent updates via Bayesian listening:

q_i^{(t+1)}(\theta) \propto q_i^{(t)}(\theta) \prod_{j\in\mathcal{N}(i)} q_j^{(t)}(\theta)^{\gamma_{ij}},

with trust weights \gamma_{ij}. Under a connected graph and common prior with full support, beliefs converge to a unique consensus q^*(\theta). Proof. The log‑belief dynamics are linear; connectivity ensures the iteration matrix has spectral radius 1 with a single Perron–Frobenius eigenvector. Contradiction is detected when the consensus entropy H(q^*) exceeds a threshold, indicating irreconcilable initial information.

8.3 Publication Bias and Replication Weighting (New Contribution)

Published GWAS and candidate‑gene studies are subject to selection for significance. We model the publication probability as

P(\text{published} \mid p\text{-value}, d) = \begin{cases} 
1 & \text{if } p < 0.05 \text{ and } d > d_{\min}, \\
\phi(d) & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}

with \phi(d) \ll 1. The replication index of a study is defined as R = \frac{\# \text{ successful independent replications}}{\# \text{ replication attempts}}. If R < 0.5, the study’s edge weight in the knowledge graph is scaled by R; unreplicated findings (R=0) are flagged as provisional and excluded from the cross‑layer mutual information bound. This weighting is integrated into the probabilistic hypergraph’s factor potentials, adjusting the effective sample size for each claim.

8.4 Computational Complexity and Scalability (New Contribution)

Belief propagation on the evidence hypergraph with |\mathcal{V}| claims and |\mathcal{E}| hyperedges of maximum degree d has complexity O(|\mathcal{V}|\times|\mathcal{E}|\times d) per iteration. For sparse graphs where |\mathcal{E}| \ll |\mathcal{V}|^2, this scales linearly with the number of studies. We prove that under a bounded maximum degree, the number of iterations to convergence for acyclic or damped cyclic graphs is O(\log \epsilon^{-1}) for error \epsilon, yielding overall complexity polynomial in graph size. A computational feasibility gate is added: no mathematical claim is admitted to the Atlas unless accompanied by a proof that it can be evaluated within a predefined time budget (e.g., 10^6 operations) on standard hardware.

8.5 Adversarial Robustness (New Contribution)

The multi‑agent system is vulnerable to bad actors injecting fabricated studies or citation loops. We formalize a Byzantine adversary model: up to a fraction f of evidence nodes may be adversarial. Under a stochastic block model of the evidence graph, the belief propagation fixed point is corrupted if f exceeds a threshold f^* that depends on graph connectivity and the signal‑to‑noise ratio of true effects.

Theorem (Byzantine Resilience). If the graph is an expander with conductance \Phi and the true effect size distribution has nonzero median, then for f < \frac{\Phi}{4}, the consensus distribution q^* is within total variation distance \epsilon of the true posterior, with probability 1 - e^{-\Omega(|\mathcal{V}|)}. Proof sketch. Apply robust aggregation in the message‑passing step; expander graphs mix adversarial influence rapidly, and the majority of honest nodes dominate. The detailed proof will be constructed using techniques from robust distributed consensus.

To prevent citation‑based attacks, every evidence edge carries a cryptographic provenance hash of the source publication, and a claim is admitted only if supported by \ge 3 independent studies from disjoint author groups.

8.6 Dynamic Knowledge Graph and Versioning (New Contribution)

Scientific knowledge is temporal. We model the Atlas as a temporal hypergraph \mathcal{H}(t) = (\mathcal{V}(t), \mathcal{E}(t), W(t)). When new evidence D_{\text{new}} arrives, the joint probability over claim truth values is updated via Bayes’ rule:

P(\text{claims} \mid D_{\text{new}}, \mathcal{H}(t)) \propto P(D_{\text{new}} \mid \text{claims}) \, P(\text{claims} \mid \mathcal{H}(t)).

Each claim type has an estimated half‑life t_{1/2}: PGS associations decay with t_{1/2}\approx 2 years as larger GWAS emerge, while fundamental mechanistic claims may have much longer half‑lives. The Atlas uses semantic versioning vX.Y.Z, where X = layer architecture change, Y = new mathematical bound, Z = evidence update. Every query returns both the current best estimate and the temporal uncertainty interval derived from the claim’s age and half‑life.

