9. Cross‑Layer Uncertainty Propagation with Feedback (Original, Extended)

We replace the simplistic Markov chain with a causal model allowing feedback (e.g., experience‑dependent gene expression). Let the layered variables be X_0 = \text{DNA}, X_1, \dots, X_k = \text{Cognition}. The directed information from DNA to cognition is

I(X_0 \to X_k) \triangleq \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} I(X_0^i; X_{i+1} \mid X_1^i),

where X_0^i = (X_0,\dots,X_i) (Massey, 1990). This satisfies I(X_0;X_k) \le I(X_0\to X_k), with equality if no feedback exists.

Theorem (Feedback‑Corrected Bound). Under a causal model where each layer’s dynamics depend on its own history and possibly on all past states (including top‑down cognitive influences on gene expression via stress, learning, etc.), the mutual information satisfies

I(\text{DNA};\text{Cognition}) \le -\frac12\ln(1 - h^2_{\text{SNP}}) + \Delta_{\text{fb}} + \Delta_{\text{epi}} + \Delta_{\text{dev}},

where \Delta_{\text{fb}} is the directed information from cognition back to molecular/epigenetic layers, \Delta_{\text{epi}} is the information contributed by epigenetic state dynamics not captured by DNA sequence, and \Delta_{\text{dev}} captures the tightening due to critical periods. If feedback is negligible and epigenetic marks are stable, we recover the simple bound. Proof sketch. Decompose directed information into feedforward and feedback terms; the feedforward term from DNA to first molecular layer is bounded by the additive genetic information -0.5\ln(1-h^2_{\text{SNP}}). All subsequent terms are additive because directed information chain‑rules over time. The critical period effect is incorporated by noting that time‑dependent bottlenecks reduce the maximum information flow, leading to the inequality I(X_0;X_k) \le \max_{\tau} I(X_0;X_\tau) + \text{feedback}.

