11. Causal Identification Protocol (New Section)

We replace the binary non‑causality flag with a structured protocol. For an edge from variable X to Y to be considered causal, it must satisfy one of:

· Tier 3: Direct experimental manipulation of X with randomisation, or a formally validated do‑calculus identification from observational data.
· Tier 2: Mendelian randomization with a genetic instrument Z satisfying: (i) F>10 for relevance, (ii) Z independent of confounders U, (iii) no direct effect of Z on Y except through X. Under these assumptions, the Wald estimator identifies the causal effect: \beta_{X\to Y} = \frac{\beta_{Z\to Y}}{\beta_{Z\to X}}.
· Tier 1: All other associations.

Causal Strength Index. For a proposed mediator M in the chain G \to M \to \text{Cog}, we define

C = \frac{I(G; M; \text{Cog})}{I(G; \text{Cog})},

where I(G; M; \text{Cog}) is the interaction information. A value C close to 1 suggests M captures most of the association; the index is computed only when Tier 2+ evidence exists for each link. All edges without Tier 2+ evidence are assigned C = 0 by default.

