12. Validation, Falsification, and Benchmarking Framework (Extended)

The doctoral project will conduct multi‑modal validation:

· Layer 1: UK Biobank, COGENT consortium. Compare empirical out‑of‑sample R^2 with theoretical bounds across ancestries; compute prediction intervals for individuals to confirm the futility result. Falsification: if any PGS achieves an individual 95% prediction interval narrower than \pm 15 IQ points, the theorem in Section 3.6 must be revised.
· Layer 2/3: Single‑cell RNA‑seq from developing human brain organoids and mouse lineage tracing. Simulate stochastic gene regulation and branching processes; compare with Earth mover’s distance. For critical periods, use longitudinal neuroimaging (ABCD study) to test whether gene–brain associations peak during predicted sensitive windows. Falsification: if the association does not show a statistically significant peak at the predicted developmental stage, the critical period model is rejected.
· Layer 4/5: Fit predictive coding models to MEG/fMRI (Human Connectome Project). Compare free‑energy minimization performance against null models. Falsification: if a non‑hierarchical Kalman filter yields lower free energy than the predictive coding model, the FEP layer is rejected.
· Layer 6: Simulate multi‑agent networks on synthetic evidence with known ground truth. Measure agreement rate, time to consensus, and false contradiction rate. Inject adversarial nodes and verify the Byzantine resilience threshold. Falsification: if the system reaches consensus on a false claim under f < f^*, the robustness theorem is violated.
· Knowledge Graph: Curate a benchmark of 200 contradictory and consistent claim pairs; evaluate contradiction detection precision/recall. Test dynamic versioning by simulating a stream of new studies and measuring temporal calibration.
· Cross‑species: Compare predicted orthology‑based priors with empirical validation rates in curated databases (e.g., MGI, IMPC).

Every test is accompanied by a pre‑registered null hypothesis and test statistic. A retraction protocol is defined: if a layer’s core claim is falsified, that layer’s outputs are down‑weighted in the cross‑layer bound and flagged as “under revision.”

