13. Socioeconomic Confounding and Equity Safeguard (Formal Encoding)

Socioeconomic status (SES) is a major confounder in cognitive GWAS. We model

Y = G + \text{SES} + G\times\text{SES} + \varepsilon,

where SES is itself heritable and correlated with G due to assortative mating and population structure. Genetic nurture—the effect of parental genotype on offspring environment—creates a correlation between offspring PGS and phenotype that is not mediated by the offspring’s own genome. Formalizing this, the total covariance between offspring PGS and Y includes a term \rho_g \cdot \text{Cov}(\text{PGS}_{\text{parent}}, E_{\text{offspring}}). If not accounted for, this inflates h^2_{\text{SNP}} estimates by up to 50% for educational attainment (Kong et al., 2018).

The Atlas mandates that every association study report the SES distribution of its sample and, where possible, decompose the genetic effect into direct and nurture components. The ethical API extends its refusal criterion: any query that could be used to allocate educational resources or social benefits based on genetic scores is prohibited, regardless of its population‑level framing.

