14. Interoperability and Ontology Standards (Appendix F, Summary)

To ensure the Atlas is not a silo, we adopt:

· Gene Ontology (GO) for molecular functions,
· UBERON for anatomical structures,
· PATO for phenotypic qualities,
· A newly developed Cognitive Process Ontology (CPO) for mental operations.

Cross‑layer edges are annotated with ontology relations (e.g., “has_participant,” “regulates,” “realizes”). The knowledge graph is exported in RDF/OWL, enabling SPARQL queries and linkage with external databases (WikiPathways, Neuroscience Information Framework). An OpenAPI specification defines programmatic access.

