Appendix A: Proof of Theorem 3.6 — Individual PGS Futility

Theorem (Individual PGS Futility).
For any polygenic score \hat{Y} = \sum_j X_j \hat{\beta}_j that converges to the true additive genetic value G = \sum_j X_j \beta_j as GWAS sample size N \to \infty, the width of a 95% prediction interval for an individual's phenotype Y cannot fall below 1.96\,\sigma_Y\sqrt{1-h^2_{\text{SNP}}}. When the trait is cognitive (h^2_{\text{SNP}} \le 0.3,\;\sigma_Y \approx 15 IQ points), this lower bound is approximately 24.6 IQ points.

Assumptions.

· (A1) Y = G + R, with G the additive genetic value and R the residual (environment, non-additive genetic effects, measurement error). G and R are uncorrelated by construction of the additive model.
· (A2) The linear additive model is correctly specified for the common variants used.
· (A3) \operatorname{Var}(G) = h^2_{\text{SNP}} \sigma_Y^2 exactly.
· (A4) In the large‑sample limit with unbiased effect estimates and correct LD adjustment, \hat{\beta}_j \to \beta_j almost surely, hence \hat{Y} \to G.
· (A5) The joint distribution of (G,R) is Gaussian. (Relaxed in the remark below.)

Proof.
Under (A4), the prediction error becomes Y - \hat{Y} \to Y - G = R. The variance of the residual is \operatorname{Var}(R) = \sigma_Y^2 - \operatorname{Var}(G) = \sigma_Y^2(1 - h^2_{\text{SNP}}). This variance is independent of the GWAS sample size and constitutes the irreducible error.

By (A5), R is normally distributed (as a difference of jointly Gaussian variables), so the conditional distribution of Y given \hat{Y} is Gaussian with mean \hat{Y} and variance \sigma_Y^2(1 - h^2_{\text{SNP}}). The 95% prediction interval is therefore

\hat{Y} \pm 1.96\,\sigma_Y\sqrt{1-h^2_{\text{SNP}}}.

For cognitive traits, \sigma_Y \approx 15 and h^2_{\text{SNP}} \le 0.3, giving

1.96 \times 15 \times \sqrt{0.7} \approx 24.6.

∎

Strengthening remark.
If the Gaussian assumption (A5) is violated and R has heavier tails (e.g., due to rare variant effects, §3.7), the 95% interval becomes even wider. The theorem therefore provides a conservative lower bound, reinforcing the ethical argument against individual prediction.

