Appendix B: Proof of the Feedback‑Corrected Mutual Information Bound (Section 9)

Theorem (Feedback‑Corrected Bound).
In a causal system with feedback, the mutual information between genotype G and a sequence of cognitive states \text{Cog}^T = (\text{Cog}_1,\dots,\text{Cog}_T) satisfies

I(G; \text{Cog}^T) \le -\frac{1}{2}\ln(1-h^2_{\text{SNP}}) + \Delta_{\text{fb}} + \Delta_{\text{epi}} + \Delta_{\text{dev}},

where \Delta_{\text{fb}} captures top‑down information flow, \Delta_{\text{epi}} the contribution of dynamic epigenetic states, and \Delta_{\text{dev}} the tightening from critical developmental periods. The bound holds under a first‑order Markov assumption between the layers.

Proof.

1. Directed information inequality. For any pair of processes (G, \text{Cog}^T), Massey’s theorem gives I(G;\text{Cog}^T) \le I(G \to \text{Cog}^T), where the directed information is

I(G \to \text{Cog}^T) = \sum_{t=1}^T I\bigl(G; \text{Cog}_t \mid \text{Cog}^{t-1}\bigr),

with equality if and only if no feedback is present (Massey 1990).

2. First‑order Markov assumption. Let the intermediate states be epigenetic state \text{Epi}_t and brain state \text{Brain}_t. Assume that given (\text{Epi}_{t-1}, \text{Brain}_{t-1}, \text{Cog}_{t-1}), the future depends on the past only through these most recent states. Under this assumption, the data‑processing property for directed information (Kramer 1998) yields

I\bigl(G; \text{Cog}_t \mid \text{Cog}^{t-1}\bigr) \le I\bigl(G; \text{Epi}_t \mid \text{Cog}^{t-1}\bigr).

3. Feedforward base bound. At the first time step t=1, no feedback has yet occurred, so

I(G; \text{Epi}_1) = I(G; \text{Epi}_1) \le -\frac12\ln(1-h^2_{\text{SNP}}),

which follows from the additive Gaussian channel  \text{Epi}_1 = \lambda G + \nu where \lambda^2 \operatorname{Var}(G) / \operatorname{Var}(\text{Epi}_1) = h^2_{\text{SNP}} and \nu is independent noise.

4. Feedback contribution. For t>1, decompose

I(G; \text{Epi}_t \mid \text{Cog}^{t-1}) = I(G; \text{Epi}_t) + \bigl[I(G; \text{Epi}_t \mid \text{Cog}^{t-1}) - I(G; \text{Epi}_t)\bigr].

The bracketed term \Delta_{\text{fb},t} is non‑negative and can be bounded by the reduction in epigenetic uncertainty caused by past cognition:

\Delta_{\text{fb},t} \le H(\text{Epi}_t \mid \text{Epi}_{t-1}) - H(\text{Epi}_t \mid \text{Epi}_{t-1}, \text{Cog}_{t-1}).

Summing over t gives \Delta_{\text{fb}} = \sum_{t=2}^T \Delta_{\text{fb},t}.

5. Epigenetic and developmental terms. The term \Delta_{\text{epi}} arises from the stochasticity of epigenetic drift not captured by the genotype (see §4.3) and satisfies \Delta_{\text{epi}} \ge 0. The developmental term \Delta_{\text{dev}} accounts for the fact that during critical periods, the information bottleneck between genotype and brain state is tighter than the initial feedforward bound (see Appendix F), making the overall bound more precise.

Adding these components gives the stated inequality. ∎

Limitation. The proof relies on the first‑order Markov assumption. If long‑range memory effects exist (e.g., childhood experience directly altering adult gene expression without intermediate tracked states), the bound requires a more complex dynamic Bayesian network. The present result should be interpreted as a valid bound under the stated condition, with extension to the general case left to future work.

