Appendix C: Evidential Damping — Corrected Convergence Result (Section 10.2)

Claim (Spectral stability of damped belief propagation).
Consider damped message updates m^{(t+1)} = \eta\, \mathcal{F}(m^{(t)}) + (1-\eta)\,m^{(t)}. If the undamped map \mathcal{F} has a fixed point m^* and the Jacobian J_{\mathcal{F}}(m^*) has all eigenvalues \lambda satisfying \operatorname{Re}(\lambda) < 1, then there exists \eta^* > 0 such that for all 0 < \eta < \eta^*, the damped iteration is locally asymptotically stable around m^*.

Proof.
Linearising the damped map around m^* gives J_T = \eta J_{\mathcal{F}} + (1-\eta)I. The eigenvalues of J_T are \mu_k = 1 - \eta + \eta\lambda_k, where \lambda_k are the eigenvalues of J_{\mathcal{F}}. A standard result in linear stability theory states that the iteration converges locally if |\mu_k| < 1 for all k.

The condition |\mu_k|^2 = (1-\eta)^2 + 2\eta(1-\eta)\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_k) + \eta^2|\lambda_k|^2 < 1 is equivalent to

\eta \bigl(1 - 2\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_k) + \eta(|\lambda_k|^2-1)\bigr) < 0.

For sufficiently small \eta, the sign is determined by the coefficient 1 - 2\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_k). If \operatorname{Re}(\lambda_k) < 1, then for small \eta the inequality holds. The maximum allowable \eta is \eta^* = \min_k \frac{2(1-\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_k))}{|\lambda_k|^2-1} for those \lambda_k with |\lambda_k|>1; if all |\lambda_k|\le 1, any \eta\in(0,1) works. ∎

Remarks.

· This is a local, not global, convergence result. Global contraction would require additional structure (e.g., scaled diagonal dominance in Gaussian BP).
· The theorem correctly captures the empirical behaviour of damped loopy BP: it stabilises oscillations caused by eigenvalues crossing the unit circle, provided the real parts of those eigenvalues are less than 1.
· For the hypergraph factors we use, this condition is satisfied when potential functions are sufficiently smooth; it can be verified numerically for a given factor graph.

