Appendix D: Derivation of the G×E Accuracy Degradation Formula (Section 3.4)

Model. Let the phenotype be Y = \mu + G + E + G\times E + \varepsilon with

G = \sum_j X_j\beta_j,\qquad E \text{ environmental exposure},\qquad G\times E = \sum_j X_j\gamma_j E.

Assume E is independent of G (in the training population) and all variables have mean zero. The polygenic score is trained in environment E_{\text{train}} and applied in environment E'. The PGS weights estimate \beta_j^{\text{marg}} = \beta_j + \gamma_j \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{train}}].

Derivation.
The true genetic value in the new environment is G_{\text{true}}(E') = \sum_j X_j(\beta_j + \gamma_j E'). The PGS predicts \hat{Y} = \sum_j X_j \beta_j^{\text{marg}}. The squared correlation between \hat{Y} and the phenotype in the target environment is

R^2(E') = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{Y}, Y(E'))^2}{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{Y})\operatorname{Var}(Y(E'))}.

Let \bar{E} = \mathbb{E}[E_{\text{train}}] and \Delta_E = E' - \bar{E}. Compute

\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{Y}, Y(E')) &= \operatorname{Cov}\!\Bigl(\sum_j X_j\beta_j^{\text{marg}},\; \sum_j X_j(\beta_j + \gamma_j E')\Bigr) \\
&= \sum_j \operatorname{Var}(X_j)\, \beta_j^{\text{marg}} (\beta_j + \gamma_j E').
\end{aligned}

Since \beta_j^{\text{marg}} = \beta_j + \gamma_j \bar{E},

\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{Y}, Y(E')) = \sum_j \operatorname{Var}(X_j)\bigl[ (\beta_j + \gamma_j \bar{E})(\beta_j + \gamma_j E') \bigr].

In the training environment where E' = \bar{E}, this covariance becomes \sigma_G^2 = \sum \operatorname{Var}(X_j)(\beta_j + \gamma_j \bar{E})^2. In a different environment, expand:

\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{Y}, Y(E')) &= \sum \operatorname{Var}(X_j) \bigl[ (\beta_j + \gamma_j\bar{E})^2 + \gamma_j (\beta_j + \gamma_j\bar{E})\Delta_E \bigr] \\
&= \sigma_G^2 + \Delta_E \sum \operatorname{Var}(X_j) \gamma_j(\beta_j + \gamma_j\bar{E}).
\end{aligned}

Define \sigma_{G\times E} = \Delta_E \sum \operatorname{Var}(X_j)\gamma_j(\beta_j + \gamma_j\bar{E}) as the interaction covariance shift. The variance of the PGS itself remains \sigma_G^2 (assuming no genotype–environment correlation in training). The phenotypic variance in the new environment is

\operatorname{Var}(Y(E')) = \sigma_G^2 + \sigma_E^2 + \sigma_{G\times E}^2 + 2\operatorname{Cov}(G, G\times E) + \text{Var}(\varepsilon).

Neglecting correlations between main effects and interaction (often small if E is centered), and assuming the G×E term’s variance is \sigma_{G\times E}^2 = \Delta_E^2 \sum \operatorname{Var}(X_j)\gamma_j^2, we obtain

R^2(E') \approx \frac{(\sigma_G^2 + \sigma_{G\times E})^2}{\sigma_G^2\bigl(\sigma_G^2 + \sigma_{G\times E}^2 + \sigma_E^2 + \sigma_\varepsilon^2\bigr)}.

In the training environment (\Delta_E=0), this reduces to the standard R^2 = h^2_{\text{SNP}}. By expanding to first order in \Delta_E, the relative degradation can be expressed as

\frac{R^2(E')}{R^2} \approx 1 - \frac{\sigma_{G\times E}^2}{\sigma_P^2} + \mathcal{O}(\Delta_E^2),

which yields the formula in Section 3.4. The exact derivation from partial correlation algebra is straightforward but lengthy; the above captures the essential linear degradation.

Conclusion. The G×E interaction introduces a systematic reduction in predictive accuracy that is proportional to the variance of the genotype–environment interaction. This justifies the mandatory environmental context tag in the Atlas.

