Appendix E: Cross‑Population Transferability Bound — Justification of \kappa (Section 3.5)

Claim. For a polygenic score trained in population A and applied to population B,

\mathbb{E}[R^2_{A\to B}] \le h^2_{\text{SNP},A}\, r^2_{\text{LD}}(A,B)\,(1 - F_{\text{ST}}(A,B))^\kappa,

where \kappa\ge 1 depends on the genetic architecture.

Proof outline.
Let \boldsymbol{\beta}_A be the vector of true SNP effects in population A (scaled by genotype variance). The PGS weights are \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_A = \mathbf{R}_A^{-1} \mathbf{r}_A, where \mathbf{R}_A is the LD matrix and \mathbf{r}_A the marginal association vector. Under the infinitesimal model, the expected out‑of‑sample R^2 in population B is

R^2_{A\to B} = \frac{(\boldsymbol{\beta}_B^\top \mathbf{V}_B \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_A)^2}{(\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_A^\top \mathbf{V}_B \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_A)(\boldsymbol{\beta}_B^\top \mathbf{V}_B \boldsymbol{\beta}_B)},

where \mathbf{V}_B is the LD‑weighted genotype covariance in B.

The genetic correlation between populations, defined as \rho_g = \frac{\boldsymbol{\beta}_A^\top \mathbf{V}_B \boldsymbol{\beta}_B}{\sqrt{\boldsymbol{\beta}_A^\top \mathbf{V}_A \boldsymbol{\beta}_A \cdot \boldsymbol{\beta}_B^\top \mathbf{V}_B \boldsymbol{\beta}_B}}, can be shown to satisfy \rho_g^2 \le (1 - F_{\text{ST}})^\kappa with \kappa \ge 1 depending on the number of independent causal variants and the distribution of effect sizes (de Vlaming et al. 2017, Scutari et al. 2016). For a trait influenced by many small‑effect variants, \kappa \approx 1; for a trait with a few large‑effect variants that differ in frequency, \kappa is larger. The LD correlation r^2_{\text{LD}}(A,B) further attenuates the correlation between the true and estimated effect vectors. Combining these gives the bound. ∎

Flag. The exact value of \kappa cannot be derived from F_{\text{ST}} alone without knowing the genetic architecture. The Atlas will default to the conservative \kappa=1, acknowledging that the true degradation may be stronger.

