Appendix F: Critical Period Information Bound (Section 5.3)

Theorem (Critical Period Bottleneck).
For a fixed genetic variant, the mutual information with adult cognition satisfies

I(\text{SNP}; \text{Cognition}) \le \max_{\tau\in \text{critical windows}} I(\text{SNP}; \text{Brain}_\tau),

and the bound is tightest during critical developmental windows when small genetic differences produce large structural divergence.

Proof.
Consider the entire developmental trajectory as a Markov chain

\text{SNP} \to \text{Brain}_1 \to \text{Brain}_2 \to \dots \to \text{Brain}_T \to \text{Cognition}.

By the data‑processing inequality, I(\text{SNP}; \text{Cognition}) \le I(\text{SNP}; \text{Brain}_\tau) for any \tau \le T. Thus the minimum over all \tau of I(\text{SNP}; \text{Brain}_\tau) provides the tightest bound. However, the minimum information occurs when the mapping from genotype to brain state is least noisy—i.e., during critical periods where the system is highly sensitive to genetic variation. We formalise sensitivity via the Fisher information: a bifurcation point at time \tau^* corresponds to a near‑singular Jacobian of the brain dynamics, which amplifies small genetic differences and locally maximises I(\text{SNP}; \text{Brain}_\tau). Hence the maximum over critical windows gives the binding constraint. ∎

Remark. This bound can be interpreted as an information‑bottleneck view of development: the genotype‑cognition channel capacity is limited by the epoch with the smallest effective information transmission. The proof requires the Markov assumption; if feedback from later cognition to earlier brain states exists, the DPI direction must be reversed, and the bound becomes a lower bound on directed information—a topic for future work.

