Appendix G: Bayesian Multi‑Agent Consensus — Perron–Frobenius Proof (Section 8.2)

Theorem (Consensus).
Consider n agents with belief distributions q_i^{(t)}(\theta) over the same continuous parameter \theta, updating according to

q_i^{(t+1)}(\theta) \propto q_i^{(t)}(\theta) \prod_{j\in\mathcal{N}(i)} q_j^{(t)}(\theta)^{\gamma_{ij}},

with fixed trust weights \gamma_{ij}>0 forming a connected communication graph. If all agents share a common prior with full support, the belief densities converge pointwise to a unique consensus distribution q^*(\theta).

Proof.
Let s_i^{(t)}(\theta) = \log q_i^{(t)}(\theta). The update rule in log‑domain is linear:

s_i^{(t+1)}(\theta) = c_i^{(t)} + s_i^{(t)}(\theta) + \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}(i)} \gamma_{ij} s_j^{(t)}(\theta),

where c_i^{(t)} is a normalisation constant independent of \theta. Define the vector \mathbf{s}^{(t)}(\theta) = (s_1^{(t)}(\theta),\dots,s_n^{(t)}(\theta))^\top. Then

\mathbf{s}^{(t+1)}(\theta) = \mathbf{c}^{(t)} + \mathbf{W}\, \mathbf{s}^{(t)}(\theta),

with weight matrix W_{ii} = 1 and W_{ij} = \gamma_{ij} for j\in\mathcal{N}(i). By construction, \mathbf{W} is a non‑negative matrix; connectedness makes it irreducible. The Perron–Frobenius theorem guarantees a unique positive right eigenvector \mathbf{v} corresponding to the spectral radius \rho = \max(\text{eigenvalues}). Because the update includes the self‑weight of 1, the spectral radius is exactly 1 + \max_i \sum_j \gamma_{ij}. To obtain convergence, we normalise the weights so that \sum_j \gamma_{ij} = \gamma < 1 for all i; this can be achieved by scaling all \gamma_{ij} by a common factor. Then \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{I} + \mathbf{A} where \mathbf{A} is the adjacency matrix of weights, and the dominant eigenvalue is 1+\gamma with eigenvector \mathbf{1}.

Iterating, \mathbf{s}^{(t)}(\theta) converges to a vector proportional to the dominant eigenvector \mathbf{1}, meaning all agents converge to the same log‑belief up to an additive constant, i.e., s_i^{(t)}(\theta) \to s^*(\theta) + \text{const}_i. The additive constants disappear in normalisation, yielding a common posterior q^*(\theta). The consensus distribution is the geometric mean of the initial beliefs weighted by the eigenvector centralities. ∎

Contradiction detection. If initial beliefs are severely conflicting, the entropy H(q^*) will be high. A threshold on this entropy signals contradictions requiring human review.

