Appendix H: Byzantine Resilience Theorem (Section 8.5)

Theorem (Byzantine Resilience under Expander Graphs).
Assume an evidence hypergraph where each honest node presents a claim with a true latent value, and up to a fraction f of nodes are Byzantine adversaries that can broadcast arbitrary beliefs. If the communication graph between agents is an expander with conductance \Phi, and agents use a robust aggregation rule (e.g., coordinate‑wise median or trimmed mean) before the Bayesian update, then there exists a constant C>0 such that for f < \Phi/4, the consensus distribution converges to a neighbourhood of the true posterior, with total variation distance \epsilon = O(f/\Phi).

Proof sketch.
The expander property ensures that the number of edges leaving any set of Byzantine nodes is large; consequently, the influence of adversarial beliefs is diluted. Robust aggregation ensures that a small number of extreme values does not dominate. Standard results in robust distributed estimation (Su & Vaidya 2016) show that under these conditions, the honest nodes’ estimates remain within a bounded distance of the true value, with the error proportional to the fraction of adversaries divided by the graph expansion. Adapting this to the belief propagation setting yields the stated bound. ∎

Note. A full, rigorous proof requires specifying the exact robust aggregation rule and the loss function; this is a substantial independent contribution and will be developed in the thesis.

